  <preface xml:id="preface">
    <title>Preface</title>
    <p>
      Multivariable calculus studies how quantities change across a plane, through space,
      and along curves and surfaces. This book develops its formulas from geometry:
      vectors describe motion and direction, linear maps describe local change, and
      integration combines local measurements into a total.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first part builds vectors, linear algebra, and parametrizations. The next two
      parts develop differentiation and multiple integration. Vector fields, work,
      circulation, and flux then lead to Green's, Stokes', and the divergence theorems.
      The final parts introduce differential forms, explain how generalized Stokes
      unifies these integral theorems, and connect the ideas to conservation laws and
      projects. Throughout, a geometric picture is paired with the hypotheses that make
      its associated calculation valid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Worked examples demonstrate methods, checkpoints test their meaning, and exercises
      ask for calculations, explanations, and further investigation. Statements supplied
      for use are distinguished from their proofs. Material titled <q>Optional</q> offers
      additional arguments or connections and can be opened in the HTML edition when
      desired. The appendices provide targeted reviews and computational companions.
    </p>
  </preface>

  <!-- Reader prerequisite contract added during the prerequisite audit. -->
  <preface xml:id="how-to-read">
    <title>How to Read This Book</title>
    <p>
      This book assumes algebra, trigonometry, and single-variable calculus: limits,
      continuity, derivatives and their product and chain rules, the Mean Value Theorem,
      definite integrals, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, and basic substitution.
      It does not assume prior multivariable calculus, differential geometry, or exterior algebra.
      Vectors and the linear algebra used in the book are developed in Chapters 1<ndash/>3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read sections in the order presented. A worked example may introduce a new idea before
      its formal definition; the explanation supplies the tools needed for the following
      checkpoints. Looking-ahead remarks motivate later topics and do not add prerequisites
      to the current exercises. When a theorem's proof is deferred, its statement may be used
      under its stated hypotheses, but the forward reference is not a proof already given.
      The appendices are references, not additional assumed background unless a passage
      explicitly directs you to the needed explanation.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the HTML edition, select a folded heading to reveal its content. Optional
      material is identified in the heading; hints and solutions are labeled separately.
      Figures support the surrounding explanation, and interactive figures allow you to
      explore the same geometry by changing a view or a parameter.
    </p>
  </preface>
</frontmatter>
